# ===== Docker build context =====
#
# The context is the repo root, and almost none of it belongs in an image. Without this file the
# context is ~20k files of `node_modules` plus five coverage report trees, which makes the build slow
# and the cache useless.

# Dependencies and build output are produced *inside* the image, never copied in — a host's
# `node_modules` is platform- and arch-specific, and `dist/` may be stale.
node_modules/
packages/*/node_modules/
packages/*/dist/
dist/
release/
out/

# The image is built from source, so the coverage trees and test artifacts are dead weight.
# `coverage-run*` are the alternates TESTING.md §1 describes.
coverage/
coverage-*/
coverage-baseline/
test-results/
playwright-report/
e2e/

# Runtime data. `data/` holds `engine.token` — a live secret — and must never enter an image.
data/
screenshots/
*.log

# VCS and editor state.
.git/
.gitignore
.github/
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp

# Docs and planning. Useful to a human, irrelevant to `node_modules` and `dist`.
plan/
*.md
!README.md

# Platform binaries that would poison a Linux image if they ever appeared.
build/
*.exe
*.dll
*.dylib
*.node

# The Docker files themselves: changing them should not invalidate the source layers.
Dockerfile
Dockerfile.*
.dockerignore
docker-compose*.yml
